[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-37", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 37 — ZGC refuted, IC09 at its floor, the SUT CPU-bound on its host; first 64-slot row 3852 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-37.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 37 - ZGC refuted, IC09 measured to its floor, and the SUT found CPU-bound on its host\n\n> Seeds: iteration 36's ZGC trial and S28 (IC09 at full scale). Judge:\n> bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung :sf1-full; SUT levi, driver judah, LAN.\n\n## Generate / Rank\n\n  1. ZGC (one flag). Prediction: tails fall, ceilings unchanged.\n     REFUTED: 8 slots identical (1024 passes), 32 slots at 2048 FAILS at\n     66% where G1 passes -- ZGC's concurrent work takes CPU from the\n     server threads on a 10-core host; the build took 71 s against 31.\n     Reverted. (Iteration 36's tails were 3-5 ms max for the fast ops\n     once the planner recount was closed; the pause hypothesis was weak.)\n\n  2. IC09 (ic09_scale_diag, in process, 40 official parameters):\n\n       engine 10.8 ms   two-hop 8.7   expansion 3.6   twin 1,119   plain 5,393\n       two-hop persons: median 2,228, max 2,622\n\n     The drive is 500x the plain join and 120x the hand-written twin. The\n     heap of the merge (a persistent sorted-map over ~2,200 group heads)\n     was 1.6 ms: now a PriorityQueue on the JVM (datalog dc7100c2):\n     two-hop 8.7 -> 7.1, engine 10.8 -> 8.7, 0 disagreements. What\n     remains is generic two-hop expansion (3.5 ms for 2,228 bindings) and\n     touching each group once.\n\n  3. Served ceilings did not move with (2): 8 slots 1024 / 32 slots 2048\n     as in iteration 36. The 8-slot column is bound by slots x the ~2 ms\n     LAN round trip (2,116 ops/s at 8 slots is 82% of 8 / 3.1 ms mean\n     wire latency), not by the SUT. The 32-slot column was the SUT's 8\n     threads: with 16 threads on the 10-core host, 2560 reads went from\n     56% to 89.5% on-time, and 64 slots pass 2560 reads = 3852.3 ops/s\n     (3072 fails). Load average 13 on 10 cores: the SUT is CPU-bound on\n     this host.\n\n    rung sf1-full  slots  sut-threads  reads/s  upd/s  on-time%  thru/s\n      it.36           8       8        1024     1092   100.00    2115.4\n      it.37           8       8        1024     1092   100.00    2113.5   (unchanged)\n      it.36          32       8        2048     1475   100.00    3512.2\n      it.37          32      16        2048     1475   100.00    3522.2   (2560: 89.5%)\n      it.37          64      16        2560     1475   100.00    3852.3   (3072: 48.3%)  NEW\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Three experiments, one refuted, one measured to a floor, one found\n    the real bound. The M4 wall is now stated by a number: 10 cores at\n    load 13. The audited No.1 runs 160 cores. From here the road is a\n    larger host (simeon is non-resident under a fleet process; this\n    workstation pages) or less CPU per operation -- the JDK HttpServer's\n    per-request cost is unmeasured (S29).\n  - A 64-slot column is a new column, not growth on the 32-slot one; the\n    32-slot row is within noise of iteration 36. Reported as such.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: growth by column (64 slots, 3852.3)\nwith the 8- and 32-slot columns unchanged; ZGC refuted and recorded.\nRatio to the audited No.1: 36 at 64 slots.\n\nSeeds for iteration 38, in order:\n\n  - S29: measure the HTTP layer's cost per request (a no-op route) --\n    if it is a third of the CPU, the SUT's server is the next engine.\n  - M4: a host with more cores for the SUT, or the driver on two hosts.\n  - S26: a full-data parameter set. S23b: IC05. M3: SF3 (~13 GB).\n"}]
